if i told you that you were 103 pages  and less than two hours away from  improving your speaking skills would you  believe me well keep watching today  we're going to talk about a short but  excellent book that will immediately  supercharge your speaking skills in a  foreign language hey friends welcome  back to my channel for those who don't  know me yet i am luca apariello a  language coach who speaks 14 languages  on this channel i help people learn  foreign languages more efficiently  faster and in a fun way  confess to it ever since you decided to  learn a foreign language you've been  looking for that quick fix you know that  shortcut that thing that gets you fluent  faster it's not just you everybody's  looking for the same thing that's why  everywhere you look you can find tips  and methods to learn language in a year  a month a week a day and even in a  matter of hours and minutes  i hate to be the party pooper but let me  tell you straight away that these magic  pills do not exist if you start learning  a language tomorrow you will still be  learning it by next weekend next  february and the next time halley's  comment passes by unless of course you  choose not to maintain it so you can't  improve your knowledge of a language  immediately that is out of reach but if  you're looking for ways to improve your  foreign language skills immediately  those my friends are quite easily  available  and you can put them to use in your life  much much faster than you think thanks  to a little book called how to improve  your foreign language immediately by  boris chet  before we dig into our review of the  book let me present a simple metaphor  clothes i have them you have them we all  have them right now at this exact moment  in time you have a finite amount of  clothing that is yours to use as you  wish  unless you want to wear all of your  clothing at once you need a place to  store it all one option would be to keep  them all in a pile you know a big  tangled mess of socks sweaters scarves  t-shirts and tank tops all gathered  together in one corner of your bedroom  another option would be to separate and  organize it all you can hang the shirts  scarves and tank tops in your closet and  fold the scarves and pair the socks for  safekeeping deep in your dresser drawers  you can even organize each individual  item by color or season in which you  wear it  now i'm sure you'll agree that in both  scenarios you have the same amount of  clothing having a closet does not make  clothes magically appear or disappear  but let me ask you this  if i gave you 10 seconds to find a  specific pair of socks in which scenario  would you be most likely to succeed  the second of course having  organizational tools like a closet or  set of drawers will give you a higher  chance of being able to find and use the  clothes you need exactly when you need  them this is exactly what how to improve  your foreign language immediately  provides  it doesn't teach you nouns doesn't teach  you adjectives politeness forms or how  to better use the subjunctive mood  instead it gives you a set of seven  intuitive tools to help you organize and  make better use of the knowledge you  already have no matter how miniscule or  expansive that knowledge may be the book  in short teaches you how to use language  effectively  let's dig into our review  how to improve your foreign language  immediately by boris shetland is a print  book the most current edition of which  was released in 2013. the book a short  read of 103 pages is divided into nine  separate chapters one introductory  chapter seven body chapters and an  epilogue  each of the seven body chapters is  devoted to a particular communicative  tool in the other's own words  communicative tools are the combination  of skills which allow a speaker to use  most effectively the level of foreign  language in his or her command  the chapters dedicated to these  effective speaking strategies generally  contain the following a description of  the tool an example of the tool in  practice typically through a  hypothetical conversation between a  foreign speaker and a native an analysis  of the example or examples and an  exercise through which to practice the  tool in addition sprinkled throughout  the book are a series of anecdotes  personal stories taken from shekman's  experiences teaching russian to english  speakers either one-on-one or in small  groups  and in classroom settings considering  that 75 percent of the short simple book  is devoted solely to teaching you to use  those tools right away you get a lot of  value in a compact inexpensive package  of course none of that information would  matter if the tools weren't so useful  and intuitive each tool can be  immediately put to use in one of three  specific areas quantity quality of  communication show your stuff and build  up your islands communication in  difficult scenarios shifting gears  simplify simplify and breaking away and  increasing expressiveness of language  embellish it and say what these areas  are ones that all language learners seek  to improve regardless of target language  and target language proficiency as  bizarre as it seems as you read up on  each of shekman's seven tools you will  immediately understand how will make you  a better speaker of your target language  how can that be because knowingly or not  you've already used these tools to  communicate better in your own native  language you may even use several or all  of them every single day  think about it have you ever told a joke  so many times to so many people that by  the up teeth telling you know exactly  what words what pauses what intonation  and what facial expressions to get the  maximum number of laughs from your  audience that joke according to shatman  is a well-practiced island that has been  built up and refined according to tool  number two build your islands have you  ever been chatting with a new group of  people when all of a sudden  talk shifts to  a subject you know nothing about instead  of pretending you know about a field you  clearly do not you use some  conversational kung fu to coax the topic  of conversation towards some common  ground so you can resume speaking freely  again the act of definitely changing the  topic when you're out of your  conversational depth is what scheckman  would call shifting gears and is the  third tool that the book provides  have you ever tried to explain a  difficult concept to a friend or family  member and despite your efforts you've  been completely unsuccessful  instead of giving up however you choose  to simplify your words and phrasing and  you try again simplifying complex  language is an example of scheckman's  fourth tool simplify simplify and is  just as useful amongst native speakers  at as it is between natives and  foreigners you see what i mean you don't  have to have tried out all of these  tools in a language learning context to  know that they will help you communicate  better because in the end they're not  language learning tools they're  communication tools and no matter how  old you are if you're reading this  you've done enough communication already  to have an intuitive feel for what works  and what doesn't work all of shakman's  seven tools have this intuitive quality  as far as i'm concerned i've been using  a lot the island part and i normally  build islands that i work and rework for  each island i build a set of 30  sentences and i practice with my tutor  and then i write everything into my  notebook and review the sentences  occasionally i practice them in real  life they come quite in handy  with the advice in the book you have  everything you need to communicate your  current level navigate difficult  conversations with natives and speak in  a more fluid natural way given the  book's short compact form these tools  are easy to practice and review on the  fly i highly recommend this book to any  learner of any language at any level of  skill in terms of actionable skills this  book provides most bang for its buck of  any language learning book on the market  i only wish that the book were of higher  quality with more substantial references  and background material so here's my  suggestion for you guys get the book  check link in the description box read  it in a week it's short but you might  want to use it as a guide underline the  parts that are relevant and interesting  for you make a list of activities you  want to try apply what you have learned  the best way to learn is to do not just  read and last but not least jot down in  a nice lengthy comment below  about your experience your thoughts  about the book what worked and what  didn't doing and sharing are the best  ways to stay motivated and take things  to the next level as always thanks for  watching and see you in the next video  [Music]  you
 

